    <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
    <section style="margin: 2.5rem 0;" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
      <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Percentage Calculation Mistakes</h2>
      <ul style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li><strong>Dividing by the New Value Instead of the Old:</strong> The percentage change formula requires dividing by the initial baseline value (`Old`). Dividing by the `New` value will give the wrong percentage.</li>
        <li><strong>Confusing Percentage Points with Percentage Change:</strong> If tax increases from 10% to 12%, that is an increase of 2 percentage points, but a 20% relative percentage increase ((12 - 10) / 10 = 0.20).</li>
        <li><strong>Asymmetry of Increases and Decreases:</strong> A 50% drop requires a 100% gain to break even. For example, if $100 drops by 50% to $50, gaining 50% only brings it to $75, not back to $100.</li>
      </ul>
